{
  "concept": "In an Atwood machine, two unequal masses joined by one string over a frictionless pulley share a single tension and accelerate with equal magnitude in opposite directions, driven by the difference in their weights.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "beige ceiling beam at top with pulley mounting stem",
    "gray grooved pulley wheel with hub",
    "yellow cord draped over the pulley, both ends hanging vertically",
    "wood-grain square block m1 (higher, left side), labeled m1",
    "wood-grain square block m2 (lower, right side), labeled m2",
    "free-body panel labeled 'Block 1': wood-grain square, red upward tension vector T, red downward weight vector w1",
    "free-body panel labeled 'Block 2': wood-grain square, red upward tension vector T (same length as Block 1's), red downward weight vector w2 (longer than w1)",
    "green acceleration arrows on each block (a up on m1, a down on m2, equal length), shown after release"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "massDifference",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Mass m2 (m1 fixed)",
      "range": [1, 5, 0.1],
      "default": 3,
      "teaches": "The two tension arrows stay equal no matter how the masses change — only the weight arrows and the resulting acceleration grow with the mass difference, so unequal weights cause the motion while one tension acts on both blocks"
    },
    {
      "id": "releaseState",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Release the blocks",
      "states": ["held", "released", "moving"],
      "default": "held",
      "teaches": "Once released, the heavier block descends and the lighter block rises by exactly the same distance in the same time — the connecting string forces equal-magnitude, opposite-direction accelerations"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "One string, one tension",
      "narration": "Compare the two free-body diagrams: both tension arrows are identical in length because a single string carries the same tension to both blocks, while w2 is longer than w1 because m2 is heavier.",
      "state": { "massDifference": 3, "releaseState": "held" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change the mass difference",
      "narration": "Drag the m2 slider: the weight arrows and the acceleration arrows resize together, but the two tension arrows remain equal — the mass difference sets how fast the system accelerates, not whether the tensions match.",
      "state": { "massDifference": 5, "releaseState": "held" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Release and watch the motion",
      "narration": "Step from held to released to moving: m2 sinks while m1 rises by the same amount, showing the string constraint — equal-magnitude accelerations in opposite directions.",
      "state": { "massDifference": 5, "releaseState": "moving" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Balanced masses",
      "narration": "Set m2 equal to m1: the weight arrows match, acceleration drops to zero, and the blocks hang motionless even after release — acceleration comes only from the weight difference.",
      "state": { "massDifference": 1, "releaseState": "moving" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A single string enforces a single tension and a single acceleration magnitude on both blocks; only the difference in weights drives the motion, so equal masses mean zero acceleration.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Left panel: beige ceiling beam, gray pulley on a short stem, yellow cord hanging vertically on both sides to wood-grain square blocks — m1 higher on the left, m2 lower on the right. Right panel: two free-body diagrams side by side under headings 'Block 1' and 'Block 2', each a wood-grain square with a red upward T arrow from the top edge and a red downward weight arrow (w1, w2) from the bottom edge; tension arrows always drawn equal length, w2 longer than w1 by an amount tracking the slider. Acceleration arrows (distinct color, e.g. green) appear on the blocks in the released/moving states, pointing up on m1 and down on m2 with equal lengths that scale with the mass difference. In the moving state, animate block positions: m2 descends and m1 rises by equal distances, cord sliding over the pulley. Keep arrow lengths proportional and clearly comparable across the two free-body diagrams — the equal tensions versus unequal weights is the central visual comparison."
}

